    "body": "Ben Collins (left) and Johan Beal (right) Photo: Alison Koo\nBy Alison Koo\n18 November 2026\nDemand for project cargo across Asia is expected to surge in the near term, but the industry’s capacity to transport such shipments is struggling to keep pace with the rapid development of projects.\nAt Breakbulk Asia in Singapore today, MSC’s head of project cargo, Ben Collins, said the region was becoming increasingly important to the successful execution of major infrastructure and industrial projects.\n“Vietnam has an emerging nuclear power programme; Asia is very central to the supply and successful delivery of these projects, so it’s time for optimism and bullishness.”\nAAL Shipping’s commercial chief, Felix Schoeller, explained that geopolitical developments had accelerated the relocation and expansion of engineering, procurement, and construction (EPC) activities in Asia.\n“There has been a big push in the last 10 years, going back to the first Trump administration, which resulted in the China Plus One strategy, boosting manufacturing capacity across South-east Asia and India,” he said.\nHowever, some executives warned that inadequate landside infrastructure and logistical constraints could hamper the movement of oversize and heavy cargo.\nKohki Uemura, CEO of Japanese heavylift forwarder Denzai, identified road and bridge infrastructure as a major bottleneck when transporting modules weighing between 500 and 1,500 tonnes, adding: “Roads and bridges are not enough for such heavy cargo.”\nYohan Beal, Ceva Logistics’ operations director for Asia Pacific, said responsibility for addressing these challenges rested with the parties conducting logistics surveys and overseeing project requirements.\n“Is due diligence being done?” he asked. “Can I make sure that I can actually get the cargo out of the port? Can I actually deliver the cargo from the port to the final destination?\n“Those are the parameters we need to ascertain before we actually fix the port of discharge and the final route we take,” he added.\nMr Beal noted that project owners and EPC contractors were increasingly recognising the importance of incorporating logistics considerations at an early stage, leading them to involve specialist logistics providers earlier in the planning process.\n“Since the pandemic, people who used to be underappreciated in the logistics field are now making their way to the boardrooms. Clients now seek approval before purchase orders are signed,” he said.\nMr Collins echoed the call for earlier planning, cautioning project owners against prioritising short-term cost savings at the expense of future transportation requirements.\n“Don’t be dictated by looking for a cheaper price tomorrow… what you might have to move today is going to cost you more tomorrow,” he said.",
